# Era 13 — Verification & Sustaining Process (V44–V47)

_Phase 4 · CLI Documentation Closure V1_

**Status: ✅ done, verified 2026-09-17**

The roadmap's final era closes two different things: whether what got written is actually
correct (a one-time audit), and whether the gap stays closed after this roadmap ends (a
standing process). `docs.txt`'s rule 10 — "keep docs part of future changes" — is what this
era turns from an intention into a mechanism.

---

## V44 — Verified-vs-reviewed audit of every example

### Goal
Every code example across `cli/*.mdx` (post Eras 3–12) is marked either "verified: executed
against a live `beaver`" or "reviewed against source" — per Invariant 9's explicit
distinction — with none left unmarked.

### Reuses
Access to a live `beaver-cli` build and a real (or realistic test) workspace, where
available — for commands where live execution isn't practical (destructive, billing-
affecting, or requiring infrastructure this session doesn't have), "reviewed against
source" is the honest fallback, stated as such rather than implied as tested.

### Implement
- Pass over every example block added or verified in Eras 3–11; for each, either run it (if
  safe and feasible) and mark it "verified," or confirm it against the current `beaver-cli`
  source one more time and mark it "reviewed against source."
- The "needs live smoke-test" punch-list items flagged in Era 1 V3 and individual family
  eras (e.g. Era 7 V25's `service cluster upgrade`/`replace-member` consequences) are
  resolved here first, since they're the highest-value items to actually execute.
- A short marker convention (e.g. an MDX comment or a small inline note) distinguishes the
  two states consistently across every page — decided once, applied everywhere, not
  reinvented per page.

### Validate
- Zero examples left unmarked.
- Every Era-flagged "needs live smoke-test" item is either resolved (verified) or explicitly
  still marked as source-reviewed-only with a reason.

### Done when
The marking convention is applied consistently and the acceptance gate's criterion 5 is
satisfied.

---

## V45 — Disclosure boundary final sweep

### Goal
One last full-repo pass with the Era 2 checklist's named-term list (both the Era 9
infrastructure terms and the Era 11 AI terms) against every `cli/*.mdx` page — not just the
high-risk eras' pages, since a leak could plausibly land anywhere.

### Reuses
The Era 2 V5 checklist and term list; Era 9 V30's redaction-log format as the pattern for
recording any findings.

### Implement
- Grep (or equivalent search) the full `cli/` tree for the named terms and near-synonyms;
  every hit gets a manual read, not an automatic flag — the earlier eras already established
  that these words aren't automatically disqualifying (e.g. "drift" used correctly is fine).
- Any genuine leak found gets the same redact-or-relocate treatment as Era 9 V30.

### Validate
- The sweep's findings (if any) are logged with resolution, mirroring V30's artifact.
- A second reviewer (or a second, later pass by the same reviewer, fresh) confirms the
  all-clear.

### Done when
The acceptance gate's criterion 6 is met: no orchestration/routing/schema/internal-API
language findable anywhere in the published CLI reference.

---

## V46 — The CLI-change → docs-review gate

### Goal
A concrete, discoverable process so that a future command/flag/default/output change in
`beaver-cli` triggers a documentation review — closing the loop `docs.txt` rule 10 names.

### Reuses
Whatever review/PR process `beaver-cli` already uses (checked, not assumed) as the
attachment point — this roadmap doesn't invent a new process, it adds a step to the
existing one.

### Implement
- A short, concrete checklist (not a heavyweight policy document) added somewhere a
  `beaver-cli` contributor would actually see it when changing a command: a note in
  `beaver-cli`'s own `CLAUDE.md`, a PR template checkbox, or equivalent — the specific
  mechanism is chosen based on what that repo already has, verified before proposing
  something new.
- The checklist itself: "Does this change a command's syntax, flags, defaults, or observable
  output? If yes, does `mintlify/cli/<page>.mdx` still match? If the command is new, does it
  have a page (see `/features/docs/inventory/` for the family it belongs to)?"

### Validate
- The gate is actually reachable from within `beaver-cli`'s own workflow, not just
  documented in this roadmap where a CLI contributor would never see it — verified by
  checking where it was actually placed, not assuming placement equals visibility.

### Done when
The acceptance gate's criterion 8 is met.

---

## V47 — Recurring inventory refresh

### Goal
A defined cadence (not necessarily automated — a written recurrence is enough for V1) for
re-running Era 1's extraction against `beaver-cli` to catch drift the per-change gate (V46)
misses — commands changed without triggering the gate, or accumulated small drifts.

### Reuses
Era 1 V1–V4's extraction method, re-run rather than redesigned.

### Implement
- A short note (in `/features/docs/00-architecture.docs` or a dedicated process note)
  stating the intended cadence (e.g. "re-run the Era 1 extraction each time a `beaver-cli`
  minor version ships, or quarterly, whichever comes first") and who/what re-runs it.
- Explicitly out of scope for V1: building tooling to automate this extraction on a
  schedule — the manual, periodic re-run is sufficient to start; automation is a natural
  post-V1 candidate if the manual cadence proves to lag in practice.

### Validate
- The cadence is written down somewhere a future session (human or Claude) would find it
  when asked to continue this roadmap — cross-checked against where V46's gate was placed,
  so the two reinforcing mechanisms are actually discoverable together.

### Done when
The roadmap's acceptance gate is fully met (all 8 criteria in `00-architecture.docs`), and
this era's two sustaining mechanisms (V46's per-change gate, V47's periodic refresh) are
both in place — the gap `docs.txt` identified is closed, and the roadmap exists to notice if
it reopens.
